WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.786 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
In a grocery store, a shopper
selects a product from a shelf.

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:07.637 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Amazon's Just Walk Out uses
cameras and shelf sensors

00:00:07.637 --> 00:00:10.521 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
to identify the products
shoppers take or return.

00:00:10.771 --> 00:00:14.536 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A virtual store can also serve
as a research environment.

00:00:14.536 --> 00:00:18.142 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This is a walkthrough of the
Sari Sandbox retail simulation.

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:23.597 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Virtual try-on lets a shopper preview an
item, such as glasses, on their own image.

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:28.434 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A Transferable Variant Signal That
Grocery Retrieval Cannot Reach.

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:32.022 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Emmanuel G. Maminta and Rowel O. Atienza.

00:00:32.022 --> 00:00:34.799 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Artificial Intelligence Graduate Program,

00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:38.273 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
University of the Philippines
Diliman, Quezon City.

00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:44.058 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
An oral presentation at the 18th Asian
Conference on Computer Vision in Osaka.

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:49.685 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We study how a checkout system matches a
photograph to the exact item on a receipt.

00:00:49.935 --> 00:00:53.956 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A model compares a camera photo
with written catalog descriptions.

00:00:53.956 --> 00:00:56.495 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This is called multimodal product retrieval.

00:00:56.745 --> 00:01:01.704 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model encodes the photo and each
description as a list of numbers, or vector.

00:01:01.704 --> 00:01:04.702 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It ranks descriptions by cosine similarity,

00:01:04.702 --> 00:01:06.931 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
which measures how closely the vectors align.

00:01:07.181 --> 00:01:10.093 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The photograph shows
Campbell's Creamy Tomato soup.

00:01:10.093 --> 00:01:12.258 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model ranks Chicken Noodle first.

00:01:12.258 --> 00:01:15.522 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It identifies the brand
and confuses two flavors.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:22.151 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
In an earlier GroceryVision study, we
evaluated 190 image and text encoders.

00:01:22.401 --> 00:01:28.941 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For SigLIP2 at 384 pixels, the correct product
appeared among the first five candidates

00:01:28.941 --> 00:01:33.409 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
17.5 percentage points more
often than it ranked first.

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:36.382 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
An exact match requires the correct variety

00:01:36.382 --> 00:01:39.600 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
to rank first. This is the
measure called Recall@1.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.818 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
When the model ranks the wrong flavor first,

00:01:42.818 --> 00:01:45.760 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
do its image features still
distinguish the two flavors?

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:47.519 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Can differences learned

00:01:47.519 --> 00:01:51.178 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from one set of brands help
identify products from other brands?

00:01:52.568 --> 00:01:57.336 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We keep the image encoder frozen. Its
measurements of each photograph remain fixed.

00:01:57.586 --> 00:02:00.830 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We train a small classifier,
called a linear probe,

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:03.335 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
to distinguish varieties within one brand.

00:02:03.335 --> 00:02:06.236 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This example uses three varieties of almonds.

00:02:06.486 --> 00:02:08.209 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
On separate test photos,

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:14.219 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
the classifier reaches 97.1 percent
balanced accuracy with SigLIP2 features.

00:02:14.219 --> 00:02:16.822 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each variety counts equally within its brand.

00:02:17.072 --> 00:02:21.530 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The image features retain distinctions
that the comparison with text can miss.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:27.210 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The control uses the same training
photographs. Only their variety labels change.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:32.398 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We shuffle labels within each brand. Each
label still appears twice in this example.

00:02:32.648 --> 00:02:36.734 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The test photo keeps its original
label. We fit the directions again

00:02:36.734 --> 00:02:40.061 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
using these shuffled training
groups and compare the results.

00:02:40.781 --> 00:02:44.775 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With the original labels, each
point represents one photograph,

00:02:44.775 --> 00:02:47.416 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
and its position reflects
the features of that image.

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:50.196 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We average the points for each product. We

00:02:50.196 --> 00:02:53.522 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
then find the center of the
product averages within each brand.

00:02:53.772 --> 00:02:58.422 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
An arrow from that center to a product shows
how the variety differs from its brand.

00:02:58.672 --> 00:03:03.717 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each arrow becomes a row of a table. Each
column corresponds to one image feature.

00:03:04.437 --> 00:03:07.818 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Consider a ruler passing through
the center of the arrows.

00:03:07.818 --> 00:03:10.890 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Mark the nearest point on
the ruler for each arrow tip.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.223 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The spread of the marks
changes as the ruler turns.

00:03:14.223 --> 00:03:16.591 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The first direction gives the greatest spread.

00:03:16.841 --> 00:03:20.413 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The next direction captures
the most remaining variation

00:03:20.413 --> 00:03:22.089 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
at a right angle to the first.

00:03:22.809 --> 00:03:27.267 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Singular value decomposition finds
these directions in the full table.

00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:32.317 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The first matrix is called U. Each column
gives the weights for one direction.

00:03:32.317 --> 00:03:35.965 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The sign records which side of
the center each variety lies on.

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:38.785 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Sigma gives the strength of that direction.

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:43.414 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each row of V transpose gives the
direction in the original image features.

00:03:43.664 --> 00:03:48.603 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We retain the 64 strongest directions.
Together they form the variant subspace.

00:03:49.323 --> 00:03:52.384 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We fit these directions
using the training brands.

00:03:52.384 --> 00:03:54.645 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The fit excludes all Starbucks photos.

00:03:54.895 --> 00:03:58.831 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We project the photo and each
description onto those directions.

00:03:58.831 --> 00:04:01.273 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This measures both along the same axes.

00:04:01.273 --> 00:04:04.665 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We scale the arrows to the same
length and compare their angles.

00:04:04.915 --> 00:04:07.613 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Ten test photos show Sumatra coffee.

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:11.731 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Their average image vector scores
highest against the Sumatra description.

00:04:11.981 --> 00:04:15.003 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
After we refit the directions
with shuffled labels,

00:04:15.003 --> 00:04:17.933 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Pike Place ranks first
for the same ten photos.

00:04:18.653 --> 00:04:20.924 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We repeat the experiment ten times.

00:04:20.924 --> 00:04:25.042 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each time we fit the directions on
one set of brands and test on others.

00:04:25.292 --> 00:04:32.307 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For SigLIP2, correct training labels
improve Recall@1 by 13.6 percentage points

00:04:32.307 --> 00:04:36.150 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
over shuffled labels across
all 409 descriptions.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.158 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
When every candidate belongs to the
correct brand, the gain is 3.9 points.

00:04:41.408 --> 00:04:44.959 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Most of the gain comes from
fewer matches to the wrong brand.

00:04:44.959 --> 00:04:48.778 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The original, full embedding gives
the highest retrieval accuracy.

00:04:49.498 --> 00:04:53.988 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
On new brands, the largest gain came
from fewer matches to the wrong brand.

00:04:53.988 --> 00:04:56.634 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Checkout also requires the exact product.

00:04:56.884 --> 00:05:00.990 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
These are the same Sumatra photographs
and the same two descriptions.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.891 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With shuffled training labels,
Pike Place ranks first.

00:05:04.141 --> 00:05:07.778 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
With correct labels from other
brands, Sumatra ranks first.

00:05:07.778 --> 00:05:09.794 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The image features stayed fixed.

00:05:09.794 --> 00:05:14.445 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Changing how we compared them with text
changed which roast the model identified.
